    "final_text": "I've built the todo app, and all 79 tests pass with `npm test`. It has no dependencies (Node 18+) and uses Node's built-in test runner. I also ran the real CLI by hand, and every command and error case behaved as expected. I haven't committed anything.\n\n## Files\n- `index.js`: the command-line entry point.\n- `lib/todo.js`: all the logic (reading and writing the file, the four commands, and the `list` output).\n- `package.json`: `npm test` runs the tests.\n- `test/todo.test.js`: tests the core functions directly.\n- `test/cli.test.js`: runs `node index.js` as a real process in a fresh temp directory for each test.\n- `README.md` and a `.gitignore` entry for `todos.json`.\n\n## Behaviour\n```\n$ node index.js list\n1. [x] Buy groceries\n2. [ ] Walk the dog\n```\n- **Errors** go to stderr with exit code 1 and never change the saved file. This covers missing text or id, invalid ids (`abc`, `0`, `-1`, `1.5`), ids that don't exist, extra arguments, and unknown commands.\n- **Empty list:** prints \"No todos yet.\" along with a hint on how to add one.\n- **Completing a todo that's already done** prints a note and exits 0, since nothing is wrong.\n- **A damaged `todos.json`** gets a clear error and is left as is, so no data is lost.\n\n## Choices I made on my own\n- **IDs are never reused.** The file stores a `nextId` counter next to the todos, so deleting the newest todo doesn't free up its id. Without the counter, `delete 3` followed by `add` would create a different todo with id 3. The downside is that `todos.json` is `{ \"nextId\": \u2026, \"todos\": [...] }` rather than a plain list, though a plain list is still accepted when reading.\n- **Unquoted text works:** `add Buy milk` is the same as `add \"Buy milk\"`. Surrounding spaces are trimmed.\n- **Safe saving:** changes are written to a temporary file that then replaces `todos.json`, so an interrupted save can't leave the file half-written.\n- **Blank file:** an empty `todos.json` is treated as an empty list.\n- **Help:** `help`, `--help` and `-h` print the usage message.",
